  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure builds from south-central Canada into the\nGreat Lakes and upper Ohio Valley regions, leading to northeast\nsurface winds locally ushering in lower dewpoints and keeping \nthe forecast dry and pleasant through the remainder of the \nholiday weekend. A good deal of sunshine is forecast both Sunday\nand Labor Day, with lower humidity levels and temperatures \nhovering around seasonable levels (upper 70s to around 80).\n\n500mb heights rise after Monday as a northeast mid-level trough\nopens and departs, allowing a central CONUS ridge to shift east\nacross the Ohio Valley. This will lead to a warming trend for \nTuesday and Wednesday, with temperatures climbing back above\nseasonal levels (mid/upper 80s) and continued dry weather. The \nupper ridge flattens and shifts over the southern/southeastern \nCONUS in response to a deepening trough over the upper Great \nLakes and southern Canada. This may allow a cold front to push \ninto the region and serve as a focus for our next round of \nshowers and thunderstorms Wednesday night into Thursday.",
